From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 18:01:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 73/73] fix(refinery): find-work scans gc.routed_to=<self>, not
 assignee alone (gcp-mi9t)

mol-refinery-patrol's find-work step scanned by assignee only, so a work bead
carrying `gc.routed_to=<rig>/gastown.refinery` with no assignee was invisible
to the merge queue: the patrol legitimately concluded "no work", wrote IDLE,
and exited while ready work sat routed to it (observed th-lm5n ~72min, th-albz
~31min, both rescued only by a witness nudge naming the bead ids). For a POOL
role that shape self-heals -- the supervisor's scale check spawns a claimer
which assigns the bead to itself -- but the refinery is a singleton patrol with
no such path, so nothing in the town ever converted routed -> assigned for it.

find-work now runs a second, deliberately narrow query when the assignee scan
comes back empty: `--no-assignee` plus `--metadata-field gc.routed_to=<self>`,
keeping the existing `--exclude-type=epic`, `--has-metadata-key=branch` and
`--status=open,in_progress` filters, then converts the bead to assigned work
and clears the routing hint. `--no-assignee` is what keeps a routing hint from
being read as ownership: a bead routed here but held by an operator or a
polecat mid-implementation stays theirs.

Two deviations from the fix as written on the bead, both deliberate:

  * The claim is `--assignee="$GC_AGENT"`, NOT `gc bd update --claim`. `--claim`
    writes $BEADS_ACTOR, which is the runtime session name
    (gastown__refinery-gc-xxxx), not the canonical role identity that every
    other query in this formula and in the refinery prompt scans on. Claiming
    that way would give the bead an assignee the primary query cannot match, so
    a crash mid-merge would re-hide it behind a dead session's name -- the exact
    invisibility this change removes. Atomicity is recovered by reading the
    claim back and dropping the candidate unless it reports $GC_AGENT.
  * Scope is the refinery only, not "refinery, witness, deacon". Verified by
    grep: mol-witness-patrol.toml and mol-deacon-patrol.toml have no
    assignee-based find-work scan at all (their only --assignee=$GC_AGENT uses
    are wisp self-claim). Inventing a find-work step where none exists is a
    separate bead with its own evidence.

The refinery prompt's quick-reference restated the assignee half of this query
in one cell. Left alone it would have become the lossy copy an agent actually
runs -- the same drift class that once dropped gc.routed_to from the rejection
flow -- so it now points at the step, and the Startup orphan scan is labelled as
the report it is rather than a second merge path.

Validation: gastown/tests/test_refinery_find_work.sh extends the existing
harness, which extracts the shipped query between its sentinels and EXECUTES it
against a stub store. The stub now models the claiming write and the read-back
against a fixture it really mutates, plus --no-assignee and --metadata-field,
and formula placeholders are substituted from the formula's own [vars] (a
leftover {{...}} is a hard error) so the composed identity is exercised under
both this rig's "gastown." binding and the unbound default. Nine new tests;
six fail against the pre-fix formula and pass after. Full sweep green: all 11
gastown/tests/*.sh, the CI pytest set (839 passed, 7 skipped, 9483 subtests),
validate_registry.py --require-git, go test . -- and `gc lint gastown` emits
byte-identical findings before and after (26 pre-existing, none from this diff).

Follow-up left open deliberately: gcp-c4wo, named in the bead description as
needing coordination, is already closed and its half (--status=open,in_progress)
already shipped; it is not reopened here.
